# RULE 19 — RESERVE ACCOUNTS ARE OWNER-MANUAL ONLY

Owner permanent law (2026-07-21). Always apply. Extends `07-never-delete-only-add` — for reserve
accounts specifically, agents don't even **ADD**.

## Owner directive (verbatim, 2026-07-21)

> "do not bring in reserves accounts i will create manually, make this a permanent rule. i will
> craete in our own softwarem manually."

## The law (non-negotiable)

Factoring **reserve / holdback / retainage** accounts in `catalogs.accounts` are **owner-created
manually, in the TMS app, only.** No agent, migration, seed, script, or import may:

- **create** a new reserve / holdback account,
- **import** a reserve account (e.g. from QBO clone),
- **reclassify** or **re-subtype** an existing reserve account (no changing `account_type` /
  `account_subtype` / parent),
- **merge** two reserve accounts, or
- **archive / deactivate / delete** a reserve account.

The owner will create the correct reserve accounts himself, manually, in-app. Until he acts
manually in-app, the existing reserve accounts **stay untouched exactly as they are on prod** — including
the 11 QBO-clone reserve accounts with mixed subtypes (CashOnHand / Savings / UndepositedFunds /
Retainage / OtherCurrentAssets) across TRANSP / TRK / USMCA. Their inconsistent subtypes are **not** an
agent cleanup target.

## Scope

Any `catalogs.accounts` row whose purpose is a factoring reserve / holdback (names such as
`%reserve%`, `%holdback%`, `%retainage%` in the factoring context). This is a **balance-sheet-moving,
owner-only** decision — the more conservative reading wins on any doubt.

## Why this rule exists

Reserve subtype standardization and canonical-vs-archive selection is an owner balance-sheet decision
(CPA `factor_reserve_held` = Asset). A guessed reclassification silently moves the balance sheet. The
owner has ruled these are hand-built by him in-software; agents leave them alone.
